A = [
dict(n=27, slug="training", title="How Does an AI Go to School?", topic="Training",
buzz='"Training a model costs millions." "Trained on the internet." The word is everywhere. What happens during training?',
hw='A child learning to spell: they guess, a teacher marks it, they adjust, and try again. Thousands of times, they get better.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: **training** is an AI guessing, being told how wrong it was, and adjusting its knobs (write-up 26) to be a little less wrong. Repeat billions of times.',
step='For an LLM, the "test" is hiding the next word in real text and asking the AI to guess it (write-up 11). After every guess, each of its billions of knobs is nudged slightly. This needs huge amounts of text and computing power, running for weeks or months on thousands of GPUs (write-up 46).\n\nAt the end, the knobs are frozen. That frozen model is what you use.',
met='Announcements of how long and how expensive a model was to train. Privacy settings asking whether your data may be used to train future models.',
name='**Training**, also called **model training**.',
words=[("Training","teaching a model by adjusting its knobs on examples."),("Loss","how wrong the model's guesses are."),("Training run","one full training process.")],
notit='Training isn\'t happening while you chat. That\'s inference (write-up 28). Your chats only matter if they\'re later used to train the next version.',
take=["Know that training is the expensive, one-time part.","Check your vendor's policy on training with your data.","Remember the model's knowledge stops where its training data stops."],
close="Guess, get marked, adjust. Billions of times.",
q="Did you know your chats might train the next model?",
scene='A child guesses a spelling. The teacher marks it.\nThey adjust and try again.',
hook="Training is an AI doing that billions of times.",
extra="Expensive, slow, and done before you ever use it."),

dict(n=28, slug="inference", title="What Happens When You Hit Send?", topic="Inference",
buzz='"Inference costs." "Inference chips." Most of the AI bill now goes on it. Most people have never heard the word.',
hw='A student who has finished school sits an exam and answers one question. No more learning, just using what they know.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: **inference** is a trained, frozen AI answering your question. Every time you hit send, that\'s inference.',
step='The model reads your prompt as tokens (write-up 10), and then writes its answer one token at a time (write-up 11), using its frozen knobs. It doesn\'t learn from your chat.\n\nTraining happens once; inference happens billions of times a day, for every user. That\'s why cheaper, faster inference (quantization, caching, small models) gets so much attention.',
met='Every chatbot answer. "Price per million tokens" is mostly the price of inference.',
name='**Inference**.',
words=[("Inference","using a trained model to produce an answer."),("Frozen model","a model whose knobs no longer change."),("Knowledge cutoff","the date its training data ends.")],
notit='Inference isn\'t learning. If an AI seems to remember you, that\'s chat history on its desk (write-up 15) or a memory feature (write-up 67), not new training.',
take=["Expect most of your AI bill to be inference, paid per question.","Remember the AI won't learn from your corrections unless you build that in.","Use smaller or cached models where answers repeat."],
close="School is training. Every answer is an exam.",
q="Did you think the AI was learning from your chats?",
scene='You corrected the AI yesterday.\nToday it makes the same mistake.',
hook="That's inference: a frozen model answering. It isn't learning mid-exam.",
extra="Every time you hit send."),

dict(n=29, slug="pretraining", title="How Does an AI Learn Language in the First Place?", topic="Pretraining",
buzz='"Pre-trained" is the P in GPT. It\'s the most expensive step in all of AI. Pre-trained before what?',
hw='Before a doctor specialises, they spend years in general medical school learning everything: anatomy, chemistry, how to read and reason.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: **pretraining** is the AI\'s general schooling: reading a huge amount of text to learn language and knowledge in general, before any specialist training.',
step='During pretraining, the model plays the next-word game (write-up 11) across trillions of tokens from books, websites and code. It\'s the costliest step, done by only a few companies.\n\nThe result is a base model: it can continue text brilliantly but doesn\'t yet follow instructions or chat politely. That comes later with fine-tuning (write-up 30) and RLHF (write-up 32).',
met='The "P" in GPT. Model releases that offer a "base" version alongside a "chat" or "instruct" version.',
name='**Pretraining**, producing a **base model**.',
words=[("Pretraining","the first, huge general training run."),("Base model","the result: good at continuing text, not yet at chatting."),("Corpus","the body of text it learned from.")],
notit='Pretraining isn\'t something most companies do. You start from someone else\'s pretrained model and adapt it.',
take=["Don't plan to pretrain; adapt an existing model.","Ask what data a model was pretrained on; it shapes its blind spots.","Use \"instruct\" or \"chat\" versions for assistants, not base models."],
close="General school first. Specialising comes later.",
q="Did you know what the P in GPT stood for?",
scene='Before a doctor specialises,\nyears of general medical school.',
hook="Pretraining is the AI's general schooling, before any specialist course.",
extra="It's the P in GPT, and the costliest step."),

dict(n=30, slug="fine-tuning", title="How Do You Teach an AI Your Way of Doing Things?", topic="Fine-tuning",
buzz='"We\'ll fine-tune it on our data." Teams say it when an AI doesn\'t behave the way they want. What changes?',
hw='A trained chef joins your restaurant. They can cook, but not your menu or your plating. So they spend two weeks cooking your dishes, corrected by your head chef.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: **fine-tuning** is extra training on your own examples, so a general model learns your style or task.',
step='You collect examples of inputs and the outputs you want, often hundreds or thousands. The model continues training on them, and its knobs (write-up 26) shift towards your style.\n\nFine-tuning is good at teaching format, tone and repeatable tasks. It\'s weaker at adding fresh facts that change often; for those, looking them up at question time (write-up 53) usually works better.',
met='Company chatbots that answer in a consistent brand voice. Models specialised for medical notes or legal drafting.',
name='**Fine-tuning**.',
words=[("Fine-tuning","extra training on focused examples."),("Training examples","input and ideal-output pairs."),("Instruction tuning","fine-tuning to follow instructions well.")],
notit='Fine-tuning isn\'t the first fix to try. Better prompts and examples (write-up 21) cost nothing and often do the job.',
take=["Try prompts and examples first.","Fine-tune for how it should answer, not for this week's facts.","Keep a test set to check it actually improved."],
close="A good chef, taught your menu.",
q="Has your team fine-tuned when a better prompt would have done?",
scene='A trained chef joins your restaurant.\nThey can cook. Just not your menu.',
hook="Fine-tuning teaches a general AI your menu.",
extra="Great for style and format. Not for fresh facts."),

dict(n=31, slug="lora", title="How Do You Customise an AI Without Retraining It?", topic="LoRA",
buzz='"Just use LoRA." It\'s how people customise AI cheaply, from company chatbots to image styles. The name sounds like a person.',
hw='Instead of retraining the chef (write-up 30), you clip a recipe card to their apron: "for our biryani, more saffron, less salt". The chef stays the same; the card changes a few dishes. Swap cards for different restaurants.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: **LoRA** adds a small, removable add-on to a model instead of changing the whole thing.',
step='Full fine-tuning adjusts all of a model\'s billions of knobs. LoRA freezes them and trains a tiny set of extra knobs alongside, often well under 1% of the size.\n\nIt\'s much cheaper, needs less memory, and you can keep many small add-ons for different tasks or customers and switch between them.',
met='Image apps that learn your face or art style from a few photos. Open-model communities sharing small style add-ons.',
name='**LoRA**, low-rank adaptation.',
words=[("Adapter","a small add-on trained for a task."),("LoRA","a popular, efficient kind of adapter."),("Frozen weights","the original knobs, left unchanged.")],
notit='LoRA isn\'t a different model. It only works on top of the model it was trained for. Swap the base model and the add-on usually won\'t fit.',
take=["Ask whether a LoRA add-on would do before full fine-tuning.","Keep separate add-ons for separate tasks.","Retest when the base model changes."],
close="Same chef, different recipe card.",
q="Have you used a LoRA without knowing it?",
scene='Don\'t retrain the chef.\nClip a recipe card to their apron.',
hook="LoRA is that recipe card: a small, swappable add-on to an AI.",
extra="Cheap, small, and easy to switch."),

dict(n=33, slug="constitutional-ai", title="Can an AI Follow a Written Set of Principles?", topic="Constitutional AI",
buzz='"Constitutional AI." It sounds like AI with a parliament. It\'s one of the ways AI companies teach models to behave.',
hw='Give a student a short rulebook: "be helpful, be honest, don\'t help anyone cause harm". Ask them to write an answer, check it against the rulebook, and rewrite it if it breaks a rule.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: **constitutional AI** trains a model to critique and improve its own answers using a written list of principles.',
step='RLHF (write-up 32) relies on lots of human ratings. Constitutional AI, introduced by Anthropic in 2022, uses a written set of principles, the "constitution", and has the AI itself judge which answers follow them better. Those AI judgements then train the model.\n\nIt makes the rules visible and reduces how much harmful content human raters need to see.',
met='Claude was trained with this approach. Published model "constitutions" and behaviour guidelines from AI companies.',
name='**Constitutional AI**.',
words=[("Constitution","a written list of principles."),("Self-critique","the model reviewing its own answer."),("RLAIF","reinforcement learning from AI feedback.")],
notit='It isn\'t a legal constitution, and it doesn\'t make a model perfectly safe. It\'s one training method among several.',
take=["Read a model provider's published principles before choosing it.","Write down your own AI's principles, even informally.","Test behaviour; principles on paper aren't proof."],
close="A written rulebook, used to check its own work.",
q="If you wrote a constitution for your AI, what would rule 1 be?",
scene='Give a student a rulebook: helpful, honest, harmless.\nWrite, check, rewrite.',
hook="Constitutional AI teaches a model to check its own answers against written principles.",
extra="The rules are written down, not just implied."),

dict(n=36, slug="data-labelling", title="Who Teaches the AI What's Right?", topic="Data labelling",
buzz='"Data labelling." "Human in the loop." Behind every smart AI, there are people marking examples. It\'s rarely in the headlines.',
hw='Show a computer 10,000 photos. Someone has to mark each one: "cat", "dog", "neither". Without those marks, the computer can\'t learn which is which (write-up 3).\n\nThat\'s the hello world: **data labelling** is people (or tools) attaching the right answers to examples so AI can learn from them.',
step='Labels can be simple ("spam") or detailed: drawing boxes around cars, transcribing speech, rating which of two chatbot answers is better (write-up 32). Big AI projects use thousands of labellers, often through specialist companies.\n\nLabel quality is model quality. Inconsistent or biased labels teach the AI inconsistent or biased behaviour.',
met='"Select all squares with traffic lights" puzzles, which helped label images. Thumbs up and down buttons in chat apps.',
name='**Data labelling**, also called **annotation**.',
words=[("Label","the right answer attached to an example."),("Annotator","the person adding labels."),("Gold set","a carefully checked set of labels used for testing.")],
notit='Labelling isn\'t unskilled busywork. Medical, legal and safety labels need experts, and clear guidelines matter as much as the people.',
take=["Write clear labelling guidelines with examples.","Have two people label a sample and compare.","Budget for labelling; it's often the hidden cost of AI."],
close="Someone has to mark the answers first.",
q="Did you know those traffic-light puzzles helped train AI?",
scene='10,000 photos. Cat, dog, or neither?\nSomeone has to mark each one.',
hook="Data labelling: people marking the right answers so AI can learn.",
extra="Label quality is model quality."),

dict(n=37, slug="overfitting", title="Why Can an AI Ace the Practice Test and Fail the Real One?", topic="Overfitting",
buzz='"The model is overfitting." Data scientists say it often. It\'s one of the oldest problems in machine learning.',
hw='A student memorises the exact answers to last year\'s exam paper. They score 100% on that paper. Then the real exam has different questions, and they fail.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: **overfitting** is an AI memorising its training examples instead of learning the general pattern.',
step='A model can get so good at its training data that it learns the noise too: accidental details that won\'t repeat. It looks brilliant in testing on familiar data and disappoints on new cases.\n\nThe fix is to always test on data it has never seen, use more varied examples, and stop training before it starts memorising.',
met='AI projects that worked perfectly in the demo and stumbled on real customers. Benchmark scores that don\'t match real-world performance (write-up 97).',
name='**Overfitting**.',
words=[("Overfitting","memorising examples instead of learning patterns."),("Test set","data held back to check the model fairly."),("Generalisation","doing well on new, unseen cases.")],
notit='Overfitting isn\'t the model being too smart. It\'s the model learning the wrong lesson: the examples, not the pattern.',
take=["Always test on data the model never saw.","Be suspicious of near-perfect scores.","Use varied, real examples, not just clean demo cases."],
close="Memorising last year's paper isn't learning the subject.",
q="Have you seen an AI shine in the demo and stumble in real life?",
scene='A student memorises last year\'s exam paper. 100%.\nThe real exam has new questions.',
hook="That's overfitting: memorising examples instead of learning the pattern.",
extra="Always test on data it has never seen."),

dict(n=39, slug="emergent-abilities", title="Can Bigger AI Suddenly Learn New Tricks?", topic="Emergent abilities",
buzz='"Emergent abilities." The idea that bigger models suddenly gain skills nobody trained for. It\'s exciting, and debated.',
hw='A child learns words, words, more words... and then one day, they tell a joke. Nobody taught joke-telling directly; it appeared once enough pieces were in place.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: **emergent abilities** are skills that seem to appear in AI models only once they\'re big enough, without being trained for specifically.',
step='Researchers noticed that small models failed completely at some tasks, like multi-step arithmetic, while larger models suddenly did well. It looked like a switch flipping at a certain size.\n\nLater research argued some of these jumps depend on how you measure: with a more gradual score, improvement looks smoother. So it\'s partly real, partly a measurement effect.',
met='Headlines about AI "unexpectedly" learning to translate, code or reason as models grew.',
name='**Emergent abilities**.',
words=[("Emergence","a new behaviour appearing from many simple parts."),("Scale","model size, data and compute (write-up 38)."),("Threshold","the size at which a skill seems to appear.")],
notit='Emergence doesn\'t mean the AI is secretly conscious or unpredictable in every way. And it doesn\'t guarantee new skills will keep appearing as models grow.',
take=["Be careful with \"it suddenly learned\" headlines.","Test new models on your own tasks; abilities differ.","Don't plan on future emergent skills; plan on what works today."],
close="Enough words, and one day: a joke.",
q="Do you think AI skills appear suddenly, or gradually?",
scene='A child learns word after word.\nThen one day, they tell a joke.',
hook="Emergent abilities: skills that seem to appear once an AI is big enough.",
extra="Real, debated, and partly about how we measure."),

dict(n=40, slug="model-collapse", title="What Happens When AI Learns From AI?", topic="Model collapse",
buzz='"Model collapse." As AI-written text floods the internet, researchers worry future AIs will learn mostly from AI. What goes wrong?',
hw='Photocopy a photo. Then photocopy the copy. Then the copy of the copy. After 20 rounds, details fade and odd smudges grow.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: **model collapse** is quality slowly degrading when AI models are trained mostly on content made by other AIs.',
step='Each AI generation slightly over-represents common patterns and loses rare ones. Train the next model mostly on that output, and the losses compound: answers get blander, rare facts vanish, errors repeat.\n\nResearch suggests mixing in real human data and filtering carefully prevents most of it. That\'s why fresh, human-made data has become valuable (see synthetic data, write-up 35).',
met='Debates about AI-generated text and images filling the web, and AI companies licensing human-written content.',
name='**Model collapse**.',
words=[("Model collapse","quality loss from training on AI-made data."),("Data provenance","knowing where data came from."),("Human data","content created by people.")],
notit='It doesn\'t mean AI is about to collapse. It\'s a risk that careful data mixing largely avoids.',
take=["Keep track of which data is AI-made.","Mix in real data whenever you train or fine-tune.","Value your organisation's human-written content."],
close="A photocopy of a photocopy fades.",
q="How much of what you read online do you think is AI-written?",
scene='Photocopy a photo. Then copy the copy.\nAfter 20 rounds, details fade.',
hook="Model collapse is AI learning from AI, and fading the same way.",
extra="Real human data keeps it sharp."),
]
